<h2>Part of a staple-spice basket, not a standalone trade</h2>
<p>Cumin's roughly USD 92 million in Indian exports to Bangladesh in 2024 doesn't exist in isolation — it sits alongside onion, lentils, turmeric and chickpeas as part of the same structural relationship: short transit, low freight cost relative to landed price, and deep, established buyer familiarity with Indian supply. If you already buy other staples or spices from us for the Bangladesh market, cumin is a natural addition to an existing shipping relationship rather than a new one to set up.</p>

<h2>Consolidation is genuinely practical here</h2>
<p>Because turmeric, cumin and other spices commonly ship together to the same Bangladesh buyers, there's real practical value in telling us your full spice requirement upfront rather than ordering product by product. One consolidated shipment against a combined specification is often simpler and more cost-efficient than several separate smaller orders running the same route.</p>

<h2>Routing follows the same logic as our other Bangladesh trade</h2>
<p>Sea to Chattogram (2-5 days from Indian east-coast ports) suits container-volume orders; land ports such as Petrapole-Benapole can be faster for goods sourced closer to the eastern border, though cumin sourced from Gujarat and Rajasthan — well to the west — typically makes sea freight the more practical default. Tell us your volume and we'll confirm.</p>

<h2>Payment planning is identical to our other Bangladesh lines</h2>
<p>Letters of credit dominate this market regardless of product, and LC opening and amendment time is the scheduling variable that most often catches buyers out. Plan your cumin shipment timeline from LC-operative date rather than order-confirmation date, exactly as you would for onion, lentils or any other Bangladesh order.</p>

